WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.625
One coefficient has many contours.

00:00:02.625 --> 00:00:04.518
Pick one Legendre degree, n.

00:00:04.518 --> 00:00:10.144
The coefficient measures how much of that polynomial occurs in the function on the red interval.

00:00:10.144 --> 00:00:13.999
The surrounding ellipse gives us another way to measure the same number.

00:00:13.999 --> 00:00:17.406
Watch the contour move out while the coefficient stays put.

00:00:17.406 --> 00:00:19.895
The estimate above it has three ingredients.

00:00:19.895 --> 00:00:28.870
The largest allowed function value sets a scale, the perimeter measures how far we integrate, and the kernel supplies a power that shrinks with degree.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:33.237
Lesson four, Bandtlow’s Lemma one point seven, gives us the contour.

00:00:33.237 --> 00:00:37.839
Lesson five, Lemma one point eight, gives us the decaying kernel waves.

00:00:37.839 --> 00:00:42.355
Together they prove the coefficient estimate in Lemma one point nine.

00:00:42.355 --> 00:00:44.166
Start on the real interval.

00:00:44.166 --> 00:00:48.021
Take the function one divided by two and a half minus x.

00:00:48.021 --> 00:00:49.529
Choose degree two.

00:00:49.529 --> 00:00:54.002
Its Legendre polynomial is one half of three x squared minus one.

00:00:54.002 --> 00:00:58.817
Multiply these two functions and sweep the signed area from minus one to one.

00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:02.737
The central region subtracts area and the two ends add it back.

00:01:02.737 --> 00:01:09.578
Multiplying the result by five halves gives a coefficient of about zero point zero four nine four two.

00:01:09.578 --> 00:01:12.943
Its definition uses only real inputs.

00:01:12.943 --> 00:01:15.159
A circle draws each inner ellipse.

00:01:15.159 --> 00:01:18.594
Choose the outer circle of radius rho in the z plane.

00:01:18.594 --> 00:01:27.981
Its image under the Joukowski map is the boundary of the outer ellipse, where our example is bounded, and the theorem assumes boundedness inside.

00:01:27.981 --> 00:01:32.611
Choose the smaller circle of radius s, with s between one and rho.

00:01:32.611 --> 00:01:36.175
The cyan spoke has length s and turns with the point z.

00:01:36.175 --> 00:01:41.850
Its image, the gold point omega, runs along the boundary of the inner ellipse, E sub s.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.808
At angle zero, omega is at the right tip.

00:01:44.808 --> 00:01:47.447
At a quarter turn, omega is at the top.

00:01:47.447 --> 00:01:54.935
The subscript s records which circle produced the ellipse, while its horizontal and vertical semiaxes have different lengths.

00:01:54.935 --> 00:01:59.431
The entire closed inner ellipse lies inside the open outer one.

00:01:59.431 --> 00:02:02.137
The nearest point gives the largest value.

00:02:02.137 --> 00:02:05.331
For our example, the cross at two and a half is the pole.

00:02:05.331 --> 00:02:10.445
The size of the function is one over the distance from the moving point to that cross.

00:02:10.445 --> 00:02:14.435
Trace the outer ellipse until the moving point reaches its right tip.

00:02:14.435 --> 00:02:18.638
The mint segment goes from the centre to this tip along the real axis.

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:22.052
Its length, a sub rho, is the horizontal semiaxis.

00:02:22.052 --> 00:02:29.178
To find it, apply Joukowski at angle zero, where z equals rho and its reciprocal equals one over rho.

00:02:29.178 --> 00:02:34.171
The gold distance from the tip to the pole is therefore two and a half minus a sub rho.

00:02:34.171 --> 00:02:39.590
Interior points approach that tip, so its reciprocal is the supremum in the open ellipse.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:45.081
At outer radius two point one five, this gives about zero point eight three nine.

00:02:45.893 --> 00:02:48.088
Recover one real value first.

00:02:48.088 --> 00:02:51.580
Fix x on the red interval inside the moving contour.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.792
The Cauchy kernel has its pole at x.

00:02:53.792 --> 00:02:59.845
Turn once counterclockwise around that pole, weighting the kernel by the boundary values of the function.

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:02.888
Cauchy's formula recovers the value at x.

00:03:02.888 --> 00:03:09.816
Now let x run along the interval, weight these recoveries by the second Legendre polynomial, and integrate.

00:03:09.816 --> 00:03:13.671
Collecting their kernels gives the second-kind kernel Q two.

00:03:13.671 --> 00:03:19.681
With the coefficient normalization, a factor of five remains outside the contour integral.

00:03:19.681 --> 00:03:21.705
Let the tangent turn the product.

00:03:21.705 --> 00:03:23.981
One angle chooses the boundary point.

00:03:23.981 --> 00:03:28.569
As the point turns, the pink tangent gives its direction and speed per radian.

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:31.123
At the right tip the tangent points straight up.

00:03:31.123 --> 00:03:32.801
At the top it points left.

00:03:32.801 --> 00:03:40.440
Now multiply the function value, the second-kind kernel and this tangent to obtain the violet vector in the output plane.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:44.366
Dividing by i turns that vector clockwise by a quarter turn.

00:03:44.366 --> 00:03:52.957
Multiplying its length by five over two pi gives the gold arrow, the rate of change of the accumulated integral per radian.

00:03:52.957 --> 00:03:54.476
Halve the angular step.

00:03:54.476 --> 00:03:58.722
Fix the starting angle at thirty degrees and hold its gold rate fixed.

00:03:58.722 --> 00:04:02.946
Sweep the cyan arc through four tenths of a radian on the inner ellipse.

00:04:02.946 --> 00:04:08.302
Multiplying the starting rate by the angle step gives the gold straight-line prediction.

00:04:08.302 --> 00:04:14.596
The true increment integrates the changing rate all along the arc, giving the mint arrow in the output plane.

00:04:14.596 --> 00:04:16.815
Now halve the angle step to two tenths.

00:04:16.815 --> 00:04:24.645
The predicted arrow halves, and the gap between its tip and the integrated tip falls from about sixteen thousandths to four thousandths.

00:04:24.645 --> 00:04:26.545
Halve the step again to one tenth.

00:04:26.545 --> 00:04:28.636
The gap is now below one thousandth.

00:04:28.636 --> 00:04:34.219
These small directed increments add head to tail to form the full contour integral.

00:04:34.219 --> 00:04:36.648
Two contours add different increments.

00:04:36.648 --> 00:04:41.527
Keep the same function and use two inner radii, one point five four and two.

00:04:41.527 --> 00:04:43.889
Start both running integrals at zero.

00:04:43.889 --> 00:04:46.742
Turn both boundary points through the same angle.

00:04:46.742 --> 00:04:52.410
Their radii differ, so they sample different function values, kernel values and tangents.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:56.713
The tips in the output plane trace the accumulated complex integrals.

00:04:56.713 --> 00:04:58.243
Stop at a quarter turn.

00:04:58.243 --> 00:05:00.157
The two tips are visibly apart.

00:05:00.157 --> 00:05:04.524
A partly traversed contour still remembers which ellipse we chose.

00:05:04.524 --> 00:05:06.975
Both circuits give the same coefficient.

00:05:06.975 --> 00:05:09.678
Complete the remaining three quarters of each circuit.

00:05:09.678 --> 00:05:13.917
Watch the cyan and pink traces come together on the real axis.

00:05:13.917 --> 00:05:20.439
Both reach the real integral from our first chapter, approximately zero point zero four nine four two.

00:05:20.439 --> 00:05:21.713
Why do they meet?

00:05:21.713 --> 00:05:27.168
The product of the function and the kernel is holomorphic in the region between the two contours.

00:05:27.168 --> 00:05:31.385
Deforming one contour into the other preserves its complete integral.

00:05:31.385 --> 00:05:35.901
Now we can choose any inner contour to estimate that same coefficient.

00:05:36.727 --> 00:05:38.985
Straighten the contour contributions.

00:05:38.985 --> 00:05:41.752
Follow four successive arcs of the inner ellipse.

00:05:41.752 --> 00:05:46.532
For each arc integrate the complex density, including its oriented tangent.

00:05:46.532 --> 00:05:51.972
The four coloured output arrows are the resulting increments, delta one through delta four.

00:05:51.972 --> 00:05:53.396
Join them head to tail.

00:05:53.396 --> 00:06:01.034
The gold displacement measures the distance from the first tail to the last tip, about zero point zero four nine four two.

00:06:01.034 --> 00:06:03.979
Now straighten the four arrows, preserving their lengths.

00:06:03.979 --> 00:06:10.571
The violet total is about zero point three one five because the directed arrows had partly cancelled.

00:06:10.571 --> 00:06:17.249
The mint bar measures the length of the entire curved output path, including the bends within each of the four arcs.

00:06:17.249 --> 00:06:24.774
On each arc the chord is at most the path length, so adding the four chords gives at most the integral of the absolute density.

00:06:24.774 --> 00:06:27.054
Replace the local heights by ceilings.

00:06:27.054 --> 00:06:33.455
At each angle, taking the modulus changes the contour density into a product of three real heights.

00:06:33.455 --> 00:06:38.341
Violet is the magnitude of the function at the moving point on the inner ellipse.

00:06:38.341 --> 00:06:42.822
Its horizontal ceiling is the supremum over the outer ellipse, M rho.

00:06:42.822 --> 00:06:47.174
Pink is the magnitude of the degree two kernel on that same inner contour.

00:06:47.174 --> 00:06:50.012
Its ceiling is the maximum around that contour.

00:06:50.012 --> 00:06:53.960
Mint is the speed, the distance along the ellipse per unit angle.

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:58.440
Multiply speed by a small angle step to obtain a small arc length.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:03.262
Summing those lengths around one turn gives the mint area, the perimeter L sub s.

00:07:03.262 --> 00:07:09.535
Take the first two ceilings outside the integral and keep the perimeter, giving the bound below for any degree n.

00:07:09.535 --> 00:07:12.843
This is the supremum estimate of Lemma one point nine.

00:07:12.843 --> 00:07:20.111
Lesson seven uses Lemma one point ten, where Cauchy Schwarz instead separates two angular mean squares.

00:07:20.111 --> 00:07:22.285
Every second frequency survives.

00:07:22.285 --> 00:07:26.445
Switch from the degree two contour example to the kernel of degree three.

00:07:26.445 --> 00:07:32.483
Lesson five leaves frequencies four, six, eight, and then every second positive index.

00:07:32.483 --> 00:07:38.777
The numbers c four, c six and c eight are the lengths of the first three complex arrows on this contour.

00:07:38.777 --> 00:07:40.634
Each length has two ingredients.

00:07:40.634 --> 00:07:48.272
Sigma is the coefficient found by integrating the third Legendre polynomial of cosine t against sine of k t.

00:07:48.272 --> 00:07:52.262
At frequency four this integral is thirty-two over thirty-five.

00:07:52.262 --> 00:08:00.860
Multiply it by the circle radius one point five four to the power minus four to get about zero point one six two five five.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:05.042
The identical rule gives the pink and violet lengths for six and eight.

00:08:05.042 --> 00:08:10.014
The remaining exponential has modulus one and rotates each arrow clockwise.

00:08:10.014 --> 00:08:17.908
Above each dial the wave shows the real projection of that arrow on the ellipse, enlarged separately so all three waves are visible.

00:08:17.908 --> 00:08:21.087
A full turn gives four, six and eight oscillations.

00:08:21.087 --> 00:08:26.158
Adding their lengths and all the later lengths bounds the complete kernel at any angle.

00:08:26.158 --> 00:08:28.246
Sum the geometric majorant.

00:08:28.246 --> 00:08:31.980
For degree n, the first surviving frequency is n plus one.

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:35.160
The index q counts steps of two, starting at zero.

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:40.515
Take absolute values of the waves and bound each sigma by two using Lesson five.

00:08:40.515 --> 00:08:44.377
The first three upper bounds are the blue, pink and violet blocks.

00:08:44.377 --> 00:08:50.479
Dividing any block by its predecessor cancels the initial power and leaves one over s squared.

00:08:50.479 --> 00:08:55.195
This number is less than one because the inner circle radius s exceeds one.

00:08:55.195 --> 00:09:00.678
The entire strip has length equal to the first block divided by one minus the ratio.

00:09:00.678 --> 00:09:08.743
Multiply the numerator and denominator by s to obtain two times s to the minus n over s minus its reciprocal.

00:09:08.743 --> 00:09:12.882
Put that kernel bound into the pink slot of the coefficient estimate.

00:09:12.882 --> 00:09:16.830
Its factor of two cancels the two in the contour normalization.

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:21.310
Group the perimeter and the remaining denominator into C infinity of s.

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:26.830
The factor s to the minus n stays visible because it controls decay with degree.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:29.470
Move the inner contour outward.

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:34.221
Fix rho at two point one five and keep the function and its outer norm fixed.

00:09:34.221 --> 00:09:37.650
The amber ellipse starts at the inner radius from the lesson.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:42.358
Its value is one point five four zero five before display rounding.

00:09:42.358 --> 00:09:44.485
Expand it toward the violet rim.

00:09:44.485 --> 00:09:50.132
Along the way, the perimeter and the gap s minus its reciprocal both change continuously.

00:09:50.132 --> 00:09:55.715
Their quotient therefore tends to the outer perimeter constant, since rho is greater than one.

00:09:55.715 --> 00:09:58.759
The coefficient stays on the left of every inequality.

00:09:58.759 --> 00:10:07.969
Taking the limit on the right gives the theorem without ever needing values of a general bounded holomorphic function on the outer boundary.

00:10:07.969 --> 00:10:10.803
Read degree ten on the logarithmic scale.

00:10:10.803 --> 00:10:14.531
The cyan curve holds the coefficients of our fixed function.

00:10:14.531 --> 00:10:17.085
The amber curve gives the inner envelope.

00:10:17.085 --> 00:10:19.382
The violet curve gives its outer limit.

00:10:19.382 --> 00:10:21.083
Read all three at degree ten.

00:10:21.083 --> 00:10:25.791
The real coefficient is about three point eight times ten to the minus seven.

00:10:25.791 --> 00:10:29.112
The inner estimate is about zero point four two three.

00:10:29.112 --> 00:10:36.401
As the amber contour approaches the violet one, the amber marker descends to about zero point zero one zero eight.

00:10:36.401 --> 00:10:38.187
The cyan marker stays put.

00:10:38.187 --> 00:10:44.368
Each step of four powers of ten on this vertical axis represents a factor of ten thousand.

00:10:44.368 --> 00:10:46.498
Estimate the perimeter in two ways.

00:10:46.498 --> 00:10:50.382
On the outer ellipse the mint curve is the speed of the boundary point.

00:10:50.382 --> 00:10:53.433
Integrating the mint height in angle gives the perimeter.

00:10:53.433 --> 00:10:59.066
Sample it at equally spaced angles and multiply the sum of the heights by the angular step.

00:10:59.066 --> 00:11:02.629
This quadrature gives a perimeter of about six point eight three.

00:11:02.629 --> 00:11:08.027
For an analytic upper bound, consider the real and imaginary components of the tangent.

00:11:08.027 --> 00:11:13.255
The squared speed is a squared times sine squared plus b squared times cosine squared.

00:11:13.255 --> 00:11:20.189
Since b is at most a, the squared speed is at most a squared, so the gold horizontal line bounds every speed.

00:11:20.189 --> 00:11:26.696
The rectangle under that ceiling has width two pi and height a, hence the perimeter is at most two pi a.

00:11:26.696 --> 00:11:33.908
Divide this perimeter bound by pi times rho minus its reciprocal, the denominator inherited from the kernel.

00:11:33.908 --> 00:11:39.796
Substituting the horizontal semiaxis for a gives the displayed upper bound for C infinity.

00:11:39.796 --> 00:11:47.541
At degree ten, the mint perimeter calculation gives about zero point zero one zero eight for the coefficient envelope.

00:11:47.541 --> 00:11:53.914
The gold elementary estimate gives about zero point zero one three for the same envelope.

00:11:53.914 --> 00:11:56.236
Follow one coefficient through the proof.

00:11:56.236 --> 00:12:01.378
On this graph, cyan follows the actual Legendre coefficients of our fixed function.

00:12:01.378 --> 00:12:08.163
At degree ten the contour integral gives the cyan point, independent of the chosen inner circle radius.

00:12:08.163 --> 00:12:14.201
Taking absolute values and bounding the kernel gives the amber envelope on the same degree axis.

00:12:14.201 --> 00:12:22.095
Its factor s to the minus n comes from the first surviving negative power and the geometric sum on the pulled back circle.

00:12:22.095 --> 00:12:25.616
Let s increase to rho, the fixed outer circle radius.

00:12:25.616 --> 00:12:30.374
The limiting envelope is violet, and the cyan coefficients still stay below it.

00:12:30.374 --> 00:12:36.220
To see why the violet curve eventually falls, compare the bounds at two consecutive degrees.

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:42.087
Their ratio is two n plus three divided by two n plus one, times one over rho.

00:12:42.087 --> 00:12:48.658
The first factor tends to one, leaving one over rho, about zero point four six five for this ellipse.

00:12:48.658 --> 00:12:56.567
This limiting contraction explains the geometric slope of the upper envelope and the guarantee of coefficient decay.

00:12:57.393 --> 00:12:59.694
Trace the proof back to its two inputs.

00:12:59.694 --> 00:13:03.549
Read the numbers here as the lemma numbers in Bandtlow’s preprint.

00:13:03.549 --> 00:13:08.791
Lesson four, Lemma one point seven, moves the real coefficient onto an ellipse.

00:13:08.791 --> 00:13:14.757
Lesson five, Lemma one point eight, tells us which kernel waves survive on its preimage circle.

00:13:14.757 --> 00:13:18.164
These are the two direct inputs to Lemma one point nine.

00:13:18.164 --> 00:13:19.928
Look one step further back.

00:13:19.928 --> 00:13:28.668
In the proof of the kernel lemma, the positive Laurent weights from Lesson one, Lemma one point one, give the surviving coefficients their sign.

00:13:28.668 --> 00:13:34.635
Real line stability from Lesson two, Lemma one point two, bounds each coefficient by two.

00:13:34.635 --> 00:13:36.250
Keep Lesson three in sight.

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:41.556
We will use its ellipse growth bound when we put the polynomial series back together.

00:13:41.556 --> 00:13:43.409
Read the ellipse from its circle.

00:13:43.409 --> 00:13:51.196
Start with the cyan point z on the smaller circle of radius s, strictly between one and the fixed outer radius rho.

00:13:51.196 --> 00:13:52.599
Its angle is theta.

00:13:52.599 --> 00:13:59.810
The Joukowski map takes it to the gold point omega on the boundary of the inner integration ellipse, E sub s.

00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:01.937
Move both points with the same angle.

00:14:01.937 --> 00:14:06.261
The function lives on the ellipse, so we read its value at the gold point.

00:14:06.261 --> 00:14:12.377
Writing that very same value as f of J of z makes it a function of the circle point.

00:14:12.377 --> 00:14:14.255
That composition is the pullback.

00:14:14.255 --> 00:14:17.001
For the contour integral, carry the tangent too.

00:14:17.001 --> 00:14:21.475
The step on the ellipse is J prime of z times the step on the circle.

00:14:21.475 --> 00:14:26.887
Composing the kernel with this same map lets us read its waves in the circle angle.

00:14:26.887 --> 00:14:28.911
See radius act on one wave.

00:14:28.911 --> 00:14:35.695
The pullback of the second kind kernel has only negative powers of z, by the expansion in Lesson five.

00:14:35.695 --> 00:14:41.392
To see what a single power does, substitute s times the exponential of i theta for z.

00:14:41.392 --> 00:14:49.158
Raising it to the power minus k gives a length of one over s to the k, and an angle of minus k times theta.

00:14:49.158 --> 00:14:51.954
Turn the point around the circle at fixed radius.

00:14:51.954 --> 00:14:54.664
The directions change and the lengths stay fixed.

00:14:54.664 --> 00:14:59.507
Now freeze theta and increase the circle radius from one point two four to two.

00:14:59.507 --> 00:15:03.753
All three arrows keep their directions, but higher powers shorten faster.

00:15:03.753 --> 00:15:07.437
At radius two, the fourth power has length one sixteenth.

00:15:07.437 --> 00:15:09.671
The sixth has length one sixty-fourth.

00:15:09.671 --> 00:15:12.714
The eighth has length one two-hundred-and-fifty-sixth.

00:15:12.714 --> 00:15:17.210
These are the lengths before multiplying by each kernel coefficient.

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:20.478
Follow the first kernel wave as degree increases.

00:15:20.478 --> 00:15:25.662
Each coloured row now represents a different Legendre degree on the same inner contour.

00:15:25.662 --> 00:15:32.767
In any one row the kernel frequencies advance by two, so the length bounds decrease by a factor of one over s squared.

00:15:32.767 --> 00:15:34.773
Look down the first column instead.

00:15:34.773 --> 00:15:41.238
For degrees two, three, and four, the first allowed powers are minus three, minus four, and minus five.

00:15:41.238 --> 00:15:48.321
Raising the degree by one therefore moves the whole geometric majorant down by a factor of one over s.

00:15:48.321 --> 00:15:53.826
The pullback exposes the negative powers, and the kernel lemma tells us which powers can occur.

00:15:53.826 --> 00:15:59.459
Summing a row and using the contour estimate gives a bound containing s to the minus n.

00:15:59.459 --> 00:16:07.581
Let the inner contour radius approach the fixed outer radius rho to obtain the coefficient bound with rho to the minus n.

00:16:07.581 --> 00:16:09.925
Take the root to read the decay rate.

00:16:09.925 --> 00:16:19.006
For a fixed outer ellipse, Lemma one point nine bounds the coefficient by a constant, times two n plus one, times rho to the minus n.

00:16:19.006 --> 00:16:21.710
Take the n-th root for positive n.

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:23.901
The root of the prefactor tends to one.

00:16:23.901 --> 00:16:28.574
The remaining factor is exactly one over rho, the mint horizontal line.

00:16:28.574 --> 00:16:32.308
Follow the violet upper envelope of the roots toward that line.

00:16:32.308 --> 00:16:37.336
The upper limit of the actual coefficient roots is therefore at most one over rho.

00:16:37.336 --> 00:16:43.460
For our fixed function, the cyan dots give actual coefficient roots at the displayed integer degrees.

00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:46.675
The ellipse supplies a ceiling on their limiting rate.

00:16:46.675 --> 00:16:48.784
Rebuild the function on the interval.

00:16:48.784 --> 00:16:51.978
A small coefficient is one part of a small term.

00:16:51.978 --> 00:16:55.428
We also need the size of the polynomial it multiplies.

00:16:55.428 --> 00:17:01.970
On the red interval, Lesson two, Lemma one point two, bounds every Legendre polynomial by one.

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:06.486
The omitted terms are thus bounded by the sum of the coefficient bounds.

00:17:06.486 --> 00:17:08.720
Increase the number of retained terms.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:15.071
Watch the partial polynomial approach the fixed function, and watch its error on a separate logarithmic scale.

00:17:15.071 --> 00:17:18.307
The geometric tail tends to zero uniformly.

00:17:18.307 --> 00:17:24.168
Completeness of the Legendre basis identifies the limit with our function on the interval.

00:17:24.168 --> 00:17:26.277
Which ellipse supplies each power?

00:17:26.277 --> 00:17:31.177
Choose the violet outer ellipse on which the function is bounded and holomorphic.

00:17:31.177 --> 00:17:36.014
Its parameter rho is the radius of the circle before the Joukowski map.

00:17:36.014 --> 00:17:42.564
Choose a smaller gold target ellipse where we evaluate the reconstructed function, with circle radius r.

00:17:42.564 --> 00:17:45.594
The marks on the red interval are their common foci.

00:17:45.594 --> 00:17:47.636
Follow the gold factor first.

00:17:47.636 --> 00:17:57.919
Lesson three, Bandtlow’s Lemma one point six, part a, bounds the size of each degree n basis polynomial throughout the gold target ellipse by r to the n.

00:17:57.919 --> 00:17:59.982
Now follow the violet factor.

00:17:59.982 --> 00:18:06.766
Lesson six, Lemma one point nine, contributes rho to the minus n from the outer coefficient estimate.

00:18:06.766 --> 00:18:10.821
Its prefactor includes the fixed outer norm and perimeter constant.

00:18:10.821 --> 00:18:19.077
Multiply the gold mode growth by the violet coefficient decay to obtain the mint ratio, r over rho to the power n.

00:18:19.077 --> 00:18:25.457
For fixed nested ellipses this ratio is below one and the resulting upper bounds have a summable tail.

00:18:25.457 --> 00:18:30.236
The uniform reconstruction estimate is a consequence of these two lemmas together.

00:18:30.236 --> 00:18:34.134
Lemma one point nine itself bounds a single coefficient.

00:18:34.134 --> 00:18:36.734
Move the gold ellipse, then hold it fixed.

00:18:36.734 --> 00:18:41.165
Keep the outer radius at two point one five and keep the same function.

00:18:41.165 --> 00:18:44.081
Begin with a gold radius of one point two nine.

00:18:44.081 --> 00:18:45.760
The ratio is six tenths.

00:18:45.760 --> 00:18:48.527
Move the gold rim closer to the violet rim.

00:18:48.527 --> 00:18:52.979
At a radius of one point nine three five, the ratio is nine tenths.

00:18:52.979 --> 00:18:56.471
The coefficients stay the same because the function is fixed.

00:18:56.471 --> 00:18:59.771
Each basis mode now has a larger allowed size.

00:18:59.771 --> 00:19:04.777
To guarantee the same accuracy on the larger ellipse, our bound needs more terms.

00:19:04.777 --> 00:19:11.641
At the outer rim the ratio reaches one and this geometric estimate gives no decay guarantee.

00:19:11.641 --> 00:19:15.136
Keep the gold target and enlarge the violet ellipse.

00:19:15.136 --> 00:19:17.946
Hold the target radius at one point two nine.

00:19:17.946 --> 00:19:20.756
Start the outer radius at one point seven two.

00:19:20.756 --> 00:19:24.205
The target occupies three quarters of that circle radius.

00:19:24.205 --> 00:19:28.380
Now expand only the violet ellipse, up to two point one five.

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:33.835
The ratio falls to six tenths, so the geometric factor decays faster with degree.

00:19:33.835 --> 00:19:37.562
The coefficients themselves are still those of the same function.

00:19:37.562 --> 00:19:45.982
Its supremum and the perimeter constant can change on a larger domain, so the full numerical bound also depends on its prefactor.

00:19:45.982 --> 00:19:52.013
We can use a larger violet ellipse only while the function remains bounded and analytic throughout it.

00:19:52.013 --> 00:19:56.658
In this example the pole at two and a half limits how far we can go.

00:19:56.658 --> 00:19:59.557
Fix the violet outer ellipse for both curves.

00:19:59.557 --> 00:20:02.686
The violet outer ellipse is E two point one five.

00:20:02.686 --> 00:20:11.192
It stays fixed for both curves and supplies the coefficient bound because the function is bounded and analytic throughout this outer ellipse.

00:20:11.192 --> 00:20:20.444
The cyan curve gives an upper bound on the error throughout the smaller inner ellipse, E one point two nine, where r divided by rho is six tenths.

00:20:20.444 --> 00:20:28.373
The pink curve gives the bound throughout the larger inner ellipse, E one point nine three five, where the ratio is nine tenths.

00:20:28.373 --> 00:20:37.987
Each bound holds throughout its target interior and, by continuity, on its boundary, since both closed targets lie inside the violet outer ellipse.

00:20:37.987 --> 00:20:42.952
Keep all three ellipse sizes fixed and increase only N, the number of terms kept.

00:20:42.952 --> 00:20:46.892
The dashed line marks the absolute tolerance of one hundredth.

00:20:46.892 --> 00:20:51.876
Both curves use the same elementary perimeter estimate on the outer ellipse.

00:20:51.876 --> 00:20:59.272
On E one point two nine, nineteen terms first guarantee this accuracy everywhere on that target using our bound.

00:20:59.272 --> 00:21:05.302
On E one point nine three five, the corresponding count is one hundred and twenty one terms.

00:21:05.302 --> 00:21:10.117
These are sufficient counts from the estimate, and the actual error can be smaller.

00:21:10.117 --> 00:21:18.260
On each fixed inner target, the upper bound for the whole tail tends to zero as N grows, because its ratio is strictly below one.

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:25.080
The identity theorem identifies each limit with the function that the series recovers on the real interval.

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:31.729
For this rational function the finite partial sums have positive uniform error on each inner target.

00:21:31.729 --> 00:21:41.622
At the violet outer radius itself, r would equal rho and the ratio would be one, so this estimate gives no uniform convergence guarantee there.

